%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level4_DigitalForensicsLab
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_4
  levelNumber: 4
  displayName: Digital Forensics Lab
  sceneName: Room4_DigitalForensicsLab
  description: Trace the evidence. Expose the scam. Compare emails, web pages and
    records before you decide.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 868639aac13a5e34aab9a889c77ee51d, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 4: DIGITAL FORENSICS LAB'
  briefingText: 'Welcome to the Digital Forensics Lab. This room has six questions:
    six case questions, each with four answer choices. They arrive in a different
    order on every attempt, and the answer choices are shuffled too, so read each
    one carefully instead of remembering positions.'
  caseNotes: []
  objectives:
  - Answer all six questions in this room.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose.
  - Score 900 or more to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: M09
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: 'You''re a student assistant at the IT help desk. A caller says: "This
      is Dean Reyes. I''m locked out and presenting in five minutes. Reset my password
      and send it to my personal email, deanreyes.personal@freemail.example. Hurry!"'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'This is <b>pretexting</b>: a made-up story that uses authority
      and time pressure to get you to skip procedure. A reset sent to a personal
      address could hand the account to an attacker. An IT worker''s duty is to follow
      verification even when it''s uncomfortable. A real dean would understand.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Do it, because the dean outranks you
    - Reset it but send the password by SMS instead
    - Give the caller your own login for now
    - 'Follow the identity-verification procedure: call back on the dean''s directory
      number, and send resets only to the official account'
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Verify first. <b>Authority plus urgency</b> is the oldest trick there
      is. Call back on the official number.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105). Supporting: Computer
      Security (BIT1225), Authentication and Access Control \u2014 Week 9: Ethics
      for IT Workers"
  - id: M10
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: 'An email titled "Revised grading sheet" has <b>Grades_Final.docm</b> attached.
      When opened, a yellow bar says: "Content is disabled. Click <b>ENABLE CONTENT</b>
      to view the grades." <b>What''s going on?</b>'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: .docm files can contain macros, which are small programs. Office
      disables them by default for safety. Attackers make the document look empty
      or blurred so you click "Enable Content", which runs their code and can install
      malware. A grades file doesn't need macros to display.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - A normal Word update
    - 'A macro malware trap: enabling content runs hidden code'
    - A corrupted file that needs repair
    - The document is just read-only
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: Macro trap. <b>Enable Content = run their code.</b> Grades don't need
      macros.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Malware and Threats"
  - id: H01
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: 'You''re your student org''s treasurer. <b>Email from:</b> "Prof. L. Santos"
      <noparse><</noparse>lsantos.adviser@freemail.example<noparse>></noparse>

      "Hi!
      As we discussed at Tuesday''s meeting, please send the venue deposit for the
      <b>Sept 14 Acquaintance Party</b> to our new account before 3 PM today. Thanks!"

      <b>Case
      file:</b> The meeting, the date and the event were all posted on the org''s
      public page. The adviser''s school address is lsantos@uphsl-binan.example.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The email uses real names, dates and events, which takes research.
      That is <b>reconnaissance</b>, the first stage of both attacks and penetration
      tests. A targeted message aimed at one person is <b>spear phishing</b>. It
      isn't clone phishing because no earlier real email was copied. The giveaways
      are the free-mail address, the "new account" and the same-day deadline.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Random spam sent to thousands of people
    - Clone phishing (a copy of an earlier real email)
    - Spear phishing built from public information gathered beforehand
    - Ransomware
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: Spear phishing. They read your <b>public page</b> first. Real details
      don't mean a real sender.
    curriculumRef: "Advanced Cybersecurity Strategies (BIT4233) \u2014 Advanced Pen
      Testing (reconnaissance) and APT Defense (TTPs)"
  - id: H02
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: "You open a \"shared document\" link. It takes you to the <b>real</b> NovaCloud
      sign-in page (novacloud.example). After you sign in, a screen appears:\n\"<b>DocuView
      Plus</b> (unverified publisher) wants to: \u2022 Read, send and delete your
      mail \u2022 Access your files at any time \u2022 Keep access when you're not
      using it\""
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: This attack doesn't steal your password. It gets <b>you</b> to give
      a malicious app permanent access. A document viewer has no reason to send or
      delete your mail. Changing your password afterwards often <b>doesn't</b> remove
      an app's access, which stays until you remove the app from your account. The
      sign-in page being real is part of the trick.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Cancel. It's a permission-granting (consent) phishing attempt. Report it and
      review your connected apps
    - Accept, because the sign-in page was real
    - Accept, then change your password afterwards to be safe
    - Accept just this once
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Cancel. The login was real, but the <b>permissions</b> were the trap.
      A viewer doesn't need to send your mail.
    curriculumRef: "Advanced Cybersecurity Strategies (BIT4233) \u2014 Cloud Security
      (\"identity management\" in SaaS)"
  - id: H03
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: "An email comes from the finance director's <b>real</b> address in an existing
      thread, and it <b>passes</b> all email authentication checks: \"Our supplier
      changed banks. Please pay today's invoice (\u20B1480,000) to the new account
      below. I'm in meetings all day, so don't call, just reply here.\""
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: When the director's account itself has been taken over, every technical
      check passes because the email really comes from that account. This is business
      email compromise. Replying goes straight to the attacker. Zero trust means
      <b>never trust, always verify</b> each request, especially a change of bank
      details, even when it comes from inside. "Don't call" is a warning sign in
      itself.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Pay it, since the email passed authentication and came from the real address
    - Reply to the email asking the director to confirm
    - Pay half now and half after confirming
    - Verify through a separate channel you already trust (the director's directory
      number and the supplier's number on file) before changing any bank details
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Verify by <b>phone, on a number you already have</b>. A real account
      can still be controlled by an attacker.
    curriculumRef: "Advanced Cybersecurity Strategies (BIT4233) \u2014 Secure Network
      Design (\"zero-trust models\")"
  - id: H04
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: After a phishing wave on campus, IT lists four findings. <b>Which should
      be fixed first?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: "Risk = <b>likelihood \xD7 impact</b>. The admin account is very
      likely to be exploited, because its password is already public and there's
      no second factor. The impact is severe: an admin email account controls everyone's
      mail. The other findings are real but lower on one or both factors. Staff training
      matters, but it doesn't close an open door right now."
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Lab PCs are one month behind on non-security feature updates
    - The email administrator account has no MFA, and its password appears in a public
      breach list
    - The spam filter sometimes flags newsletters by mistake
    - Some staff haven't attended this year's awareness seminar yet
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: 'Fix the admin account first: <b>public password + no MFA + admin access</b>
      means high likelihood and high impact.'
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Weeks 13\u201314:
      Vulnerabilities and Risk Management (\"conduct a basic risk assessment\")"
  inboxExtras:
  - id: l4_old_welcome
    channel: 1
    sender: Forensics Team Lead
    senderDetail: lead@cyberlab.example
    subject: Your first case in the lab
    body: Welcome to the forensics lab. Reported messages arrive here and on your
      phone. The evidence wall and the network analysis station hold the records
      you need - compare before you classify. The final case review terminal unlocks
      once the evidence is in.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 
    curriculumRef: 
  - id: l4_old_receipt
    channel: 1
    sender: Brightway Store
    senderDetail: orders@brightway.example
    subject: Receipt for order BW-55120
    body: 'Thanks for your order: wireless headphones, paid by card. Track it in
      the Brightway app or at brightway.example.'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 0
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 
    curriculumRef: 
  caseFiles:
  - title: Case Notes
    body: No extra records are needed in this room. Everything you need is in each
      question.
  - title: How This Room Works
    body: 'Six questions are waiting in this room. They arrive one at a time, in
      a different order on every attempt.


      Case questions have four answer
      choices, shuffled each attempt. Pick the safest and most accurate one.


      Messages
      and web pages are judged as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.


      Score 900 or more to
      unlock the next room.'
  firstMessageDelay: 10
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 10, y: 20}
  minimumActivityDelay: 5
  scoring:
    basePoints: 250
    speedBonus: 25
    fastWindow: 0.35
    wrongPenalty: 50
    recoveryPoints: 60
    baseReadingSeconds: 20
    secondsPerWord: 0.5
    secondsPerDetail: 3
    minReadingSeconds: 40
    maxReadingSeconds: 120
    caseExtraSeconds: 75
    overtimeStepSeconds: 10
    overtimePenaltyPerStep: 10
    maxTimePenalty: 60
    minCorrectPoints: 190
  oneStarScore: 600
  twoStarScore: 900
  threeStarScore: 1500
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 900
  nextLevelId: level_5
  nextSceneName: Room5_MacLab
  lessons:
  - Follow verification procedures under pressure from authority and urgency.
  - Recognize macro-enabled attachments and the "enable content" trick.
  - Understand that convincing personal details can come from public sources and
    don't prove the sender is real.
  - Read app permission requests and know that granting them can bypass passwords.
  - Verify high-risk requests through a separate channel, even when they come from
    trusted accounts.
  - Rank risks by likelihood and impact instead of by what seems most visible.
  learningSummary: 
  performanceHigh: Outstanding forensic work! Every conclusion was backed by the
    evidence.
  performanceModerate: Good investigation. Review the explanations - the records
    hold the clues that separate real from fake.
  performanceLow: Keep practising. Compare each sender, domain and request with the
    records before you decide.
